<p>But&#8230;  that&#8217;s so annoying.  Or I could do a reverse image search, but that&#8217;s so boring (and fails unless it&#8217;s a really famous picture, in my experience).  Or I could ask an LLM.</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/2025-09-19-4.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/2025-09-19-4.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="667" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-116614" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/2025-09-19-4.jpeg 1521w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/2025-09-19-4-300x238.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/2025-09-19-4-1024x813.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/2025-09-19-4-768x610.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Wut&#8230;  it tried for 1m26s and then just doesn&#8217;t want to help me at all.  I mean, it&#8217;s good that they&#8217;ve made ChatGPT refuse to identify people from images, but surely actors in movies is a special case?</p>
<p>But after poking at it a few more times, it finally coughs up the truth: It can&#8217;t download images from external URLs.</p>
<p>Thanks, Chat!</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/2025-09-19-5.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/2025-09-19-5.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="471" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-116615" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/2025-09-19-5.jpeg 1521w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/2025-09-19-5-300x168.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/2025-09-19-5-1024x575.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/2025-09-19-5-768x431.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>OK, paste in the image instead of giving it an URL, then?  OK, still refusing.  How about if I use Prompt Engineering<img src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/17.0.2/72x72/2122.png" alt="™" class="wp-smiley" style="height: 1em; max-height: 1em;" />!1!</p>
